 "answer": "Use **`pg_relation_size()`** with the index name. It returns the size in bytes of the index's main fork, which is the on-disk size of the index:\n\n```sql\nSELECT pg_size_pretty(pg_relation_size('my_vector_idx'));\n```\n\nRelated functions:\n\n- **`pg_indexes_size('table_name')`** returns the combined size of all indexes on a table.\n- **`pg_total_relation_size('table_name')`** includes the table, its indexes, and TOAST data.\n\npgvector indexes (HNSW and IVFFlat) are ordinary Postgres indexes, so these functions work on them. This answer is based on general Postgres behavior, not on any specific details of your project.",
